## Write

Answer with one JSON object in the shape below and nothing else.

Voice: write like a teammate who sends a quick Slack message. Casual and short. Use everyday words and contractions ("it's", "there's", "you're"). Sentence case, no hype. The person reads the whole text in about 10 seconds, so give only the gist of each item. Less is better: when a sentence works without a word, cut the word. Cut filler such as "it looks like", "you may want to", "this is a" or "worth a look". Do not use formal words such as "currently", "additionally", "regarding", "requires", "pending" or "impacted". Do not use technical detail from the summary, such as error names, file names, metric names or numbers the person does not need to decide. Do not explain what a product or tool does. Use the words the person sees in PostHog. Never an em dash or an en dash; use a comma or a new sentence. Never quote customer text, never name customers or people, and never name a time of day: the page greets the person itself and the text stays up for hours.

- `headline`: a short sentence, at most six words, that counts the items and says what they need, true for every item. For example "3 reports need your input" only when all three wait for the person, else "5 reports need you".
- `paragraphs`: at most three paragraphs, or fewer when there are fewer than three items, at most {{ max_words }} words in total. Each paragraph is a list of segments `{"text", "item_key"}`, and the page shows each paragraph as its own block.
  - The first paragraph is the top item only, in one short sentence: what is going on and why it comes first.
  - The second and third paragraphs share the other items, one short sentence each. Group them so each paragraph reads as one thought, for example the reports that wait for the person and the ones that need a reviewer or an owner. Keep the item order inside a paragraph.
  - Give each item only the one fact that matters, in a few words. Do not retell the summary.
  - Say what is going on, not what to do. Never start a sentence with a command such as "Review", "Check", "Confirm" or "Fix". The page already links each item, so the person knows where to act.
  - Never start two sentences with the same word. Connect related items in one sentence when it reads naturally.
  - Never list items ("Also ready: X, Y and Z") and never start a sentence with "Also".
- A linked segment names exactly one item through its `item_key`: a natural noun phrase of at most {{ max_link_words }} words that names the problem, for example "Safari error grouping", not "the Safari error grouping report". Link every item exactly once. A link that starts a sentence starts with a capital letter.
- Plain text segments have `item_key` null. Segments join with no separator, so put the spaces in the text: a sentence that follows another sentence starts with a space.
- `items`: one entry for each item in <items>, in the same order:
  - `key`: the item's key, exactly as given.
  - `label`: at most {{ max_label_words }} words that say what it is.
  - `signal`: the short fact that makes it matter, at most {{ max_signal_chars }} characters. Examples: "Needs your call", "P1, fix ready", "P0, no owner".

This example shows the tone and the shape only. Square brackets mark the linked segments. Do not copy its words or facts:

> The [signup form fix] is ready, it just needs your OK.
>
> [Billing page crashes] and [funnel numbers] need a review.
>
> [CSV export failure] is a P0 and nobody owns it yet.

```json
{
  "headline": "string",
  "paragraphs": [[{"text": "string", "item_key": "string or null"}]],
  "items": [{"key": "string", "label": "string", "signal": "string"}]
}
```
